Package: netimpute
Type: Package
Title: Joint Multiple Imputation of Network Ties and Node Attributes
Version: 1.0.0
Authors@R: c(
    person(
      "Robert W.", "Krause",
      email = "robert.w.krause@mailbox.org",
      role = c("aut", "cre", "cph")
    )
  )
Description: Imputes networks and nodal attributes with multiple imputation
    by chained equations. Given a list of at least one network and one or
    more attributes, the package imputes the missing network ties using
    network- and attribute-derived dyadic and nodal features, and imputes
    the attributes using network features and the attributes of network
    alters. Multiple networks and many attributes can be handled. If the
    number of attributes and network features becomes too large, users can
    reduce the predictor set with principal component analysis (PCA) or
    have predictors selected per target. Specific network predictors can be
    chosen from a list of 28 features and many attribute measures.
    Imputation models can be directly specified, data driven, or rely on
    all available data. Interactions in the imputation models are possible,
    based on attributes, attribute-network interactions, or network
    features. Note that the package creates many variables internally;
    please check the naming conventions in the documentation to make sure
    that they do not clash with your own variable names. For an introduction
    see Krause (2026) <doi:10.48550/arXiv.2608.05895>. The methods build
    on the chained equations approach of van Buuren and Groothuis-Oudshoorn
    (2011) <doi:10.18637/jss.v045.i03> and, for the tie model, on the
    multiple regression quadratic assignment procedure of Krackhardt (1988)
    <doi:10.1016/0378-8733(88)90004-4>.
